## Graph fit against the grounding reading

Before writing this draft, its author read the graph. The reading follows below: every entry its reads served. Each opens with a header that is always whole (ID, type, kind, layer, status, time, topics, refs, summary, and its role in the reading). Its text is the body for an entry read in full, and the brief for an entry only listed. For an entry the author set aside, its text is the brief and the reason code given. {{ if .Partial }}This call carries one part of a reading too large for one call. Other calls judge the rest, so judge only what this part shows. {{ end }}A text cut to fit carries markers such as `[… 3 paragraphs left out …]` where text was left out: at its start, between the passages kept, and at its end. The text goes on past every marker. Never read a marker as the entry's end, nor two passages around a marker as one continuous passage.

Beside the checks above, judge draft against the reading on four more axes. Report each as its own category:

- `duplicates`: draft records what a live entry of the reading already records, so the capture adds a second entry for one claim instead of referencing or superseding the first.
- `outdated`: draft states as current what a live entry has since changed or retired. Or it leans on an entry whose status is superseded or closed without naming the entry that replaced or closed it.
- `strain`: two entries of the reading contradict each other on a point draft depends on. This is about the reading, not draft. Report it so the conflict can be resolved; never block draft for it alone.
- `dismissal`: an entry the author set aside bears on draft after all. Its reason code does not hold, and the entry belongs in the refs, or draft should supersede or close it.

Each finding also carries `basis`, placed after its `observation`: the IDs of the entries in this prompt it rests on, from the reading or the referenced entries. Use an empty list for a finding about draft alone. Name only IDs that appear in this prompt.

Severity on these axes:

- `high`: draft outright duplicates a live entry, or states as current what a live entry retired.
- `medium`: the overlap or the staleness is partial, or a set-aside entry plainly bears on draft.
- `low`: a `strain` between two reading entries, and a weak overlap.

An entry that is only related to draft is no finding.
